let's talk about nucleophilic aromatic  substitution this is not your typical  reaction of aromatic compounds aromatic  compounds as we've seen before with  electrophilic aromatic substitution can  act as nucleophiles attacking an  electrophile and becoming substituted  however in certain cases nucleophiles  can actually attack the aromatic ring  let's look at an example in this example  we have this aryl chloride undergoing  substitution when treated with sodium  methoxide notice we have an electron  withdrawing group here this is going to  be critical to the mechanism and on this  side we have a leaving group  now the substitution pattern here is  important the leaving group needs to be  para or across from the electron  withdrawing group it can also have an  electron withdrawing group adjacent to  it or in the ortho position we need to  look at mechanistically why this works  and you might be tempted to draw an sn2  mechanism let's consider the sn2  mechanism for a second so our  nucleophile would have to come in a  backside attack to displace this  chloride now when we're looking at this  that means it would have to come  completely through the aromatic ring  which sterically is not possible so we'd  have to attack like this  and geometrically this just cannot occur  never do sn2 chemistry at an sp2 center  okay what about an sn1 mechanism for an  sn1 mechanism to occur this halide would  have to leave on its own now this Erol  cation is pretty high energy that  doesn't mean it's impossible to form we  see it form from aryl diazonium salts  and I have a video on that mechanism if  you're interested however hey lights are  not good enough leaving groups to  promote the formation of this aryl  cation so this is just a reaction that  cannot occur and before I throw away  these mechanisms and ask you to erase  them from your memory forever I want to  really point out sn2 is for tetrahedral  carbon and it does not work with vinyl  halides or aryl halides like we have  here then how does this mechanism occur  and why do we need electron withdrawing  groups this reaction actually occurs by  an addition elimination mechanism so our  first stage of this is adding in  methoxide and we have this extended  conjugated system going through the  entire electron withdrawing group so let  me show some arrow pushing that explains  why this can occur  first we get attack at the  carbon-bearing  the leaving group these electrons are  going to move over  and we can continue pushing all the way  through our nitro group  we'll get an intermediate that looks  like this my nitrogen here had four  bonds and so I had to add on that  positive charge and just added it back  on in this structure as well okay so  this is the addition step our methoxide  came in and utilizing this entire  conjugated system was able to add now if  we push electrons back up restoring  aromaticity we can kick out a leaving  group  and this gives our substituted product  now I showed this mechanism by pushing  electrons through the entire conjugated  system but if that's hard for you to see  I recommend drawing your mechanism to  arrows at a time and let's just do that  to break it down even a little bit more  and make sure we have our arrow pushing  correct so taking it to arrows at a time  first our nucleophile will attack here  and then we'll have to move these  electrons onto this carbon atom  this arrow pushing gives us this  compound now we can push electrons in  here making a double bond and take the  electrons from this double bond and push  them on to the carbon  that gives this resonance form and  finally still pushing two arrows at a  time we can make this a double bond and  put the negative charge on oxygen and  show how our nitro group is stabilizing  this charge just pushing two electrons  at a time that will give us this  resonance form I want to highlight this  resonance form here notice that we get  buildup of negative charge on this  carbon as well and so this explains why  an electron withdrawing group at the  ortho position - the leaving group is  also effective in promoting this  reaction we can stabilize an anion here  and notice we can stabilize an anion  here with our nitro group and we showed  all that resonance so that explains the  positioning of one or two electron  withdrawing groups on the ring let's  look at an example within ortho electron  withdrawing group to illustrate the  scope of this reaction a little more  here were reacting this Errol fluoride  with an ortho electron withdrawing group  with sodium cyanide and getting  replacement of the fluoride for cyanide  but you may be wondering why fluoride  can undergo this reaction fluorine is a  terrible leaving group in sn2 reactions  so how is this possible  well in the mechanism this step removes  aromaticity from the compound so this  makes this into something that is not  aromatic temporarily this is hard this  is the difficult step therefore that is  the rate determining step  now elimination of the leaving group is  fast it restores aromaticity in the  compound and is really favorable so  fluorines only role in the rate  determining step is being a very  electronegative element that creates a  great dipole here and makes this carbon  even more electrophilic there for  fluorine promotes this reaction very  well because of its electronegativity  and because it's poor leaving group  ability is not part of the rate  determining step this reaction actually  works great let's do a quick practice  problem to see if you've got this down  take a  look at these three substrates pause the  video if you need to and see if you can  work out which of these is most likely  to undergo nucleophilic aromatic  substitution okay so we're looking for  the substrate with an electron  withdrawing group the first one is no  good this nitrogen has a lone pair that  actually can donate electron density to  the ring by resonance this is not an  electron withdrawing group now here our  chlorine is going to make this position  more electrophilic due to its higher  electronegativity  but the bromide this is an inductive  electron withdrawing group so we do have  an electron withdrawing group here  however remember bromine also has lone  pairs and this can donate electron  density by resonance to the Ring so this  isn't the most awesome electron  withdrawing group since it just has the  inductive electron withdrawing effect  our winner is going to be the nitrile  here with the cyano group on it if we  draw up this structure even more we'll  see we have this extra conjugation so  when a nucleophile attacks we can  actually push electrons through this  entire conjugated system now if you're  having trouble figuring out which groups  are electron withdrawing and which  groups are electron donating and why I  highly recommend my video trick for  determining ewg versus EDG I hope that  you learned something and that you have  a great day today
 

